#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116124} :activity/id #uuid "0ae25db9-bfdf-5334-a08b-5c0545c2dcde" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050576 :topic/id #uuid "54fffdff-21ee-5191-90e6-7d1d444eefd6" :topic/math-academy-id 1726} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116124 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116495 :tutorial/content "An **elementary matrix** is a matrix that can be obtained from the identity matrix by applying *only one* elementary row operation.\n\nFor example, starting with the $2 \\times 2$ identity matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ we can apply a single elementary row operation to get any of the matrices below:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $E_1$ is obtained from $I_2$ by applying the row operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2.$\n\n- $E_2$ is obtained from $I_2$ by applying the row operation $R_1:=2R_1.$\n\n- $E_3$ is obtained from $I_2$ by applying the row operation $R_2 := R_2 + (-7)R_1.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "85735558-3e6a-5a6e-be0b-95227712e60a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3164 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ffae07b2-82f2-5c26-9de5-cb69f517ee2c" :step/math-academy-id 17220 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116125}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116125 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115243 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115072 :question/id #uuid "239ee94c-c3d9-5955-8cdd-afff77ec3279" :question/math-academy-id "e-4716"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "853a04b2-591a-51c2-b80f-73f559355d9f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050527}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115738} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265973} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329683} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400499} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400506}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "7e9e3e32-4737-52d8-8430-910568cdf491" :step/math-academy-id 17221 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116126}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116126 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116496 :tutorial/content "Each elementary matrix $E$ is invertible, and the corresponding inverse is always an elementary matrix of the same type as $E$.\nTo illustrate, let's consider the matrix \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the inverse of $E,$ we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1:** Write down $\\color{blue}E$ and the identity matrix $I_2$ side-by-side, as shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix. In our case, we need to apply the transformation $R_2 := R_2 + (-4)R_1.$ We apply the operation to the entire row (not only the left part):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[E\\,|\\,{I}_{2}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[{I}_{2}\\,|\\,{E}^{-1}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that [math]R_2 := R_2 + (-4)R_1[/math] transforms [math]E[/math] back into the identity matrix. Likewise, when this row operation is applied to the identity matrix, it generates the inverse of [math]E^{-1}.[/math]\n\nThe matrix $E^{-1}$ is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe can work out the inverse of all elementary $2 \\times 2$ matrices in a similar way." :tutorial/id #uuid "24a632e9-4a94-5212-b848-8d4b4403d452" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3165 :tutorial/title "The Inverse of an Elementary Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "d5bdf1c7-f166-5b10-9f1a-0aab443b2ea0" :step/math-academy-id 17223 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116127}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116127 :step/content 